Keywords: Water adsorption; Metal organic framework; Hydrophilic site; Ligand
## 1. Introduction
Recently, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention, because they have the possibility of novel functional materials such as gas adsorbents, sensors, catalysts, and photoactive materials. MOFs can be designed by selecting adhesive metal, ligand, and counter anion, and we can control the pore geometry [1–7]. Moreover, the growth from building units to MOF proceeds by self-assembly mechanism, therefore the handling to get MOF samples is simple. These points are additional merits of MOFs for future applications.
Gas sorptivity of many MOFs has been energetically researched [7–20] and some reports showed an excellent gas sorption ability which is comparable with or exceeds that of carbon materials or zeolite [20–25]. For example, Yaghi et al. reported zeolite-like MOFs that have high surface area (over $3000{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}, $ ) and porosity [20,21]. Authors showed that highly flexible $\mathtt{C u}$ -based MOF can adsorb predominant amount of methane and carbon dioxide with an unusual lattice expansion mechanism [22,26]. The high surface area and porosity would be allowed by the architecture of molecular-based linker instead of continuous atomic wall such as graphene sheet or zeolite. For the technological applications of MOFs, the stability of MOFs in an ambient condition is essentially important. Hence study on the stability of MOFs on water adsorption is desirable. Although there are many reports about MOFs with open architecture [7–26], water sorption studies are quite a few [27–30]. The reason is that many MOFs vary their structures on water sorption and furthermore many MOFs are unstable in the presence of water. Actually Greathouse and Allendorf more recently reported the interaction of water with MOF-5, showing the weakness of the secondary building unit against to water and the decrease of the surface area by the collapse of frameworks [31].
In this article, we report almost reversible adsorption and desorption characteristics of the desolvated samples of three-dimensional (3-D) MOFs $\{[\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{pzdc})_{2}(\mathrm{{pyz})]\cdot2\mathrm{{H}_{2}O\}}}}}$ (1), $\{{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}}$ $(\mathrm{pzdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}$ (2), and $\{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{pzdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpe})]{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}$ (3) (pzdc $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2\mathbf{,}3$ -pyrazinedicarboxylate, $\mathrm{pyz}=$ pyrazine, bpy $\O=$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, and bpe $\mathbf{\tau}=$ trans-1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene) with 1-D semi-rectangular pores, of which size depends on the length of the ligands [32].
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Water adsorption measurement
The adsorption measurements of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $303\mathrm{K}$ were carried out on the laboratory designed gravimetric line with a quartz spring. Prior to the adsorption measurement in order to remove adsorbed molecules, the sample was treated under the vacuum of $<10^{-2}$ Pa at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , until no further weight loss was observed. Thus-treated samples lose guest water molecules and the corresponding samples after the drying treatment of 1, 2, and 3 are noted 1a, 2a, and 3a. The samples that were characterized in the previous report were used [32,33].
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Crystal structure
Three $\mathtt{C u}$ complex samples consist of 2-D sheets of $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{C u}_{2^{-}}$ $({\mathrm{pzdc}})_{2}]_{n}$ and pillar ligands bridging neighboring sheets, and possess 3-D structure with 1-D semi-rectangular channels [32, 33], as shown in Fig. 1. The 2-D sheet $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{pzdc})_{2}]$ has no large pores through which water molecules can pass (calcd; $0.04~\mathrm{nm}\`.$ [34] and terminal oxygen atom of carboxylic group as a hydrophilic site. Pore sizes depend on the size of the used ligand and are $0.6\times0.4\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ in 1, $0.6\times0.9\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ in 2, and $0.{\overset{-}{6}}\times1.1~\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ in 3, respectively. The compounds have crystal water molecules in pore spaces and the framework structures are presented after the removal of water molecules [32,33]. Thus, they have the permanence for water.
## 3.2. Water adsorption on 1a
The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption isotherms measured at $303\mathrm{K}$ on 1a are shown in Fig. 2. The compound 1a gives the reproducible $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption isotherm, indicating that the structure of 1a is resistive against water. The shape of adsorption isotherm is categorized to type I by IUPAC classification. The type I adsorption isotherm indicates the strong interaction between 1a and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. The adsorption and desorption branches are almost similar to each other, leading to no hysteresis loop in Fig. 2a. However, the desorption isotherm shifts upward slightly in the low pressure region as shown in Fig. 2b. This indicates that a part of adsorbed water molecules are strongly bound in the 1a crystals. The adsorption uptake in the low relative pressure region is not so marked compared with that of the nitrogen adsorption isotherm [32]. The adsorbed amount increases gradually in the low pressure region and reaches the plateau region around $P/P_{0}=0.08$ , accompanying with the further increase at higher relative pressure. The adsorbed amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $P/P_{0}=0.08$ just coincides with the amount of crystal water molecules in 1 $(66.2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ). This result strongly suggests that guest water molecules are reproducibly bound to the crystal frame structures.
## 3.3. Water adsorption on 2a and 3a
The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption isotherms of 2a and $\mathbf{3a}$ at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ are shown in Figs. 3 and 4, respectively. The shape of the isotherms is similar to that of the isotherm of 1a. Both isotherms have no evident hysteresis, indicating the stable frameworks of 2a and 3a against water. Strictly speaking, the desorption branch shifts upward slightly in the high relative pressure region. Then the adsorption equilibration is not necessarily enough for determination of the correct adsorption branch in the case of compounds 2a and 3a. As the ligand as a pillar become longer, the adsorption amount is larger (up to $270\mathrm{mgg^{-1}}$ ), and in the case of 3a it is twice as large as that of 1a (Fig. 5). Interestingly, the explicit step shown in the isotherm on 1a is not observed in both isotherms. For compounds 2a and 3a, limited number of water molecules should be firstly adsorbed on the hydrophilic specific sites, and then water molecules are absorbed on the preadsorbed water molecules because of the presence of free space in the pores. Water molecules are adsorbed after the adsorption of limited number of water molecules which results the gradual increase of adsorption amount in middle pressure region. Additionally, as shown in Fig. 5, the compound with larger pore starts adsorbing water molecules in the lower pressure, indicating that the diffusion of water molecules depends on the pore size with specific sites.
## 3.4. Comparison of microporous parameters
Dubinin–Radushkevich (DR) plot is useful to analyze micropore filling of vapor.
$$
\ln W=\ln W_{0}-(A/\beta E_{0})^{2};\quad A=R T\ln(P_{0}/P).
$$
Here, $W$ and $W_{0}$ are the adsorption amount at $P/P_{0}$ and pore volume. $A$ is the adsorption potential, and $\beta$ and $E_{0}$ are affinity coefficient and characteristic adsorption energy, respectively. Moreover, filling rates were determined using the pore volume from nitrogen adsorption amounts [32]. Microporous parameters were summarized in Table 1. With the increase of pore size, the micropore volume becomes larger (Fig. 5). Heat of adsorption on the compound with smaller pores is larger because of the overlap of potential wells from pore walls. It is noteworthy that the compound with smaller pores indicates lower filling rate. As there are hydrophilic sites of carboxylic group on 2-D sheet $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{pzdc})_{2}]$ , corresponding water molecules can be adsorbed on the sites specifically. Schematic representations of water adsorption on large or small pores with hydrophilic sites for 1a and 2a or 3a, respectively, are shown in Fig. 6. In the case of 1a, at first, two water molecules per $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{pzdc})_{2}]$ unit are adsorbed on the hydrophilic sites in the $0.6\times0.4~\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ micropore. However, additional water molecules cannot enter in the small micropore because of the steric hindrance by the pre-adsorbed water molecules on hydrophilic sites. In contrast, further water molecules can be adsorbed in the pores of 3a which possesses larger size of $0.6\times1.1\mathrm{nm}^{2}$ micropore even after adsorption of water molecules on the hydrophilic sites.
The amounts of crystal water in the synthesized crystals, which are $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in 1, $\mathrm{4H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in 2, $5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in 3 are marked by dotted lines in Fig. 5. The adsorbed amount of water molecules corresponding to the crystal water is observed at similar $P/P_{0}=0.082$ . This indicates that water molecules are adsorbed as the crystal water at the same chemical potential in three compounds. The calculated value of chemical potential is $6.3\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ , suggesting that crystal water molecules are bound by the hydrogen bonding.
